  "summary": "The health-tech sector is experiencing significant momentum across multiple fronts. Major funding rounds underscore investor confidence in AI-driven healthcare solutions, with Hone Health securing $228 million for its longevity platform, Isomorphic Labs raising $5 billion for AI drug discovery, and several clinical AI startups like Aevius ($22M) and others attracting substantial capital. This capital influx reflects a broader industry trend toward integrating artificial intelligence into clinical workflows, with executives and physicians increasingly emphasizing AI's role in reducing administrative burden and improving care efficiency.\n\nRegulatory and political uncertainty continues to reshape the healthcare landscape. The FDA faces an uncertain future following the withdrawal of nominee Heidi Overton, while questions linger about the agency's direction under potential new leadership. Meanwhile, the preventive services task force is convening after a prolonged absence to discuss coverage decisions for cervical cancer, perinatal depression, and alcohol use treatments, addressing long-delayed policy questions.\n\nDrug development and therapeutic innovation remain active, with Pfizer expanding into in vivo CAR-T cell therapies through its Starna acquisition, Johnson & Johnson acquiring Ollin Biosciences for a Phase 3 eye drug candidate, and emerging startups like Marrow Therapeutics and Oyster Therapeutics raising substantial funds for blood disorders and neurodegenerative disease treatments. Additionally, the biotech landscape shows continued dynamism with Windward Bio's $225 million IPO focused on next-generation antibodies for immunology, though some programs face regulatory setbacks as exemplified by Summit Therapeutics' rejected lung cancer drug.",
